  "order": 11,
  "title": "Flats",
  "summary": "Black keys can also be named after the white key to their right, with a flat.",
  "minutes": 5,
  "steps": [
    {
      "type": "explain",
      "title": "Flat",
      "body": "A black key can also take its name from the white key on its right. **Flat** means one key lower, to the left. Its sign is ♭. The black key just to the left of B is **B flat**, written B♭.\n\n**Why it matters:** Many songs and band charts use flats: B♭, E♭ and A♭ come up all the time, especially with horn players. Knowing flats means you are never lost when someone calls one.",
      "exampleMidi": [
        71,
        70
      ],
      "labels": {
        "70": "B♭",
        "71": "B"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "show",
      "title": "The five flats",
      "body": "The same five black keys, read as flats: D♭, E♭, G♭, A♭ and B♭.",
      "highlightMidi": [
        61,
        63,
        66,
        68,
        70
      ],
      "labels": {
        "61": "D♭",
        "63": "E♭",
        "66": "G♭",
        "68": "A♭",
        "70": "B♭"
      }
    },
